    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d208">

                <pb facs="0393" n="379" xml:id="pg379"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.56/5–650</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Rusk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Special Assistant to the Secretary of Defense for Foreign Military
                        Affairs and Assistance</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Burns</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-17">July 17, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear General Burns</hi>: Reference is made to
                    Secretary Acheson’s letter of April 14, 1950 to the Secretary of Defense
                    regarding the continuation of military assistance to the Government of China,
                    and to paragraph (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) of Secretary Johnson’s reply
                    dated May 6, 1950 regarding the provision of assistance under Public Law 512,
                    79th Congress.</p>
                <p>It is the opinion of this Department, in view of recent developments in the Far
                    East and the related decisions of this Government, that the United States
                    Government should continue such assistance to the Government of China under
                    authorization of Public Law 512 as is requested by that Government and is
                    considered desirable by the Department of Defense in the light of applicable
                    military criteria.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dean Rusk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
